  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 257 AM MST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nA quieter pattern is likely to dominate the County Warning Area \n(CWA) this weekend into early next week. Upper-level flow starts off \nfrom the northwest Friday as a mini-ridge slides across the high \nplains. Ensemble guidance shows this ridge moving quickly to the \neast and weaken quite a bit heading into Saturday. Simultaneously, a \nmini-trough is anticipated to propagate across the CWA through \nSaturday, becoming more positively tilted as it makes its way east. \nThis should usher in zonal flow transitioning back to northwesterly \nas another ridge starts to influence the area on Sunday. This second \nbuilding ridge likely will be quite a bit larger than the first; \ntherefore, will be expected to influence the Tri-State area for a \nlonger period of time. Meridional flow should accompany this system \nas the ridge axis passes over our region then southwest flow likely \nfollows thereafter. Thus, dry conditions are likely to persist \nthrough early next week.\n\nDescending to the surface, guidance projects high \npressure to form late this week and last through early next week. \nThere is medium confidence on a cold front passing through Friday as \nwell as a low chance for a front late Monday as well. These should \nboth be dry passages as Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) remain \nat or below 5% through Monday. Dewpoints are forecast to be quite \ndry, ranging in the 30s and 40s throughout the long-term forecast \nperiod. Mostly sunny skies are anticipated to dominate the forecast \nperiod, outside of some cloud coverage with the frontal passage \nFriday as well as overnight into Saturday morning.\n\n\nHigh temperatures across the CWA should stay seasonably cool (mid to \nupper 50s) through the weekend until Monday where it warms into the \nmid 60s. Forecasted low temperatures will be something to keep an \neye on this week as freezing to near freezing overnight temps are \nlikely throughout the CWA this weekend and into Monday morning. \nFriday morning should be the coldest as lows around 28-32 degrees \nare possible. Fire weather should not be a big concern this weekend \nand through Monday despite Relative Humidity (RH) values forecasted \nas low as 25-30% for eastern Colorado and 35-50% for the rest of the \nTri-State area. Model guidance shows winds being fairly weak through \nMonday with 5-20 mph expected throughout the CWA. Gusts up to 30 mph \nare possible in windier locations of eastern Colorado on Saturday \nbut other than that, 15-20 mph gusts should be expected everywhere \nelse.",
